meta {
  name: 2. Honeypot triggered (silently ignored)
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/v1/send
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Origin: https://acme.example
}

body:json {
  {
    "brand": "acme",
    "name": "Spam Bot",
    "email": "bot@example.com",
    "message": "buy cheap widgets now",
    "botcheck": "i am a bot",
    "cf-turnstile-response": "test-token"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.success: eq true
}

docs {
  # Honeypot — looks like success, sends nothing

  `botcheck` is a hidden honeypot field: real users never fill it, bots that
  auto-fill every input do. When it is non-empty the Worker returns
  `200 {success:true}` **and sends no email** — the bot sees "success" and does
  not retry or adapt.

  So this request returns exactly the same 200 as a genuine submission, but no
  mail is dispatched. It short-circuits before Turnstile and SES, so it does not
  depend on any secret being configured.
}
